A||N||9||d825077||dogbone replacement||||5||
Z||000000||d825077||10-03-2000||09:59 PM||||Today I noticed that while the engine was idling it shoke.  I looked at the dogbone and it slide back and forth.  The two bolts holding it on are tight.  Is it hard to replace the dogbone?  Do I have to jack the engine up, or can I just undo the two bults and replace it?  Thank you.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||d825077||10-03-2000||10:03 PM||||I was just looking at the price of the dogbones at the fierostore.  Is it worth the extra $10 for the ultimate dog bone?  If so is it just as easy to install?  It looks bigger, like it will need wider brackets.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Songman||10-03-2000||10:22 PM||||I don't know what everyone's experience is with these things but to me it was a breeze. The dogbone does not support the engine vertically, just horizontally. I leaned across the back of the car and took the old one out. Put one bolt in the new one and moved the car by pushing it with my hips to line up the new one. It is supposed to be a direct replacement with no alterations required.<P>Is it worth the extra $10? That's a judgement call. It looks cool, and it might last a little longer but I also think the poly bushings will transfer more vibration to the car. I didn't get one for my coupe that my girlfriend drives but I will probably put one on my Formula.||||reg||1
Z||000003||contra||10-03-2000||11:30 PM||||dogbone is as easy as changing your air filter.<P>get a socket wrench out and some vice grips. You will need to remove a triangular cover on the right hand side of the dogbone with a screw driver. Vice grip the nut in there hard and then proceed to unwrench the other side of the bolt with the socket wrench. Do the same for the other bolt. BE CAREFULL not to drop the washers or the bolt, you can finger loosen the last few threads of each bolt to be more carefull if you want. Replace the bushings in the existing dogbone (who says you need another dogbone?!?) and then install it back in! You may need to push a little on the engine forward to slip the front bolt back in but that's about it. Tighten 'er up and you're good as new.<P>Estimated time of the whole ordeal is about 20 mins?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bob Englert||10-04-2000||10:33 PM||||If you have a V6, I think the Ultimate dogbone is a good choice.  It will last much longer than the stock dogbone and the V6 doesn't vibrate with the poly bushings like the 4 cyl.  <P>On my 2 4cyl cars, my 5sp has the poly bushings in the original dogbone since it normally doesn't idle under load like the auto does.  There is significantly more engine vibration transmitted to the frame with the poly bone in the 4cyl cars.  I had to remove it from my daughters 4cyl auto, way too much vibration at idle.  It did reduce the driveline slop a good deal on my 4cyl 5 sp.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||stsmithgt||10-05-2000||11:00 AM||||A suggestion would be to check out your other engine and tranny mounts as well.  If those are worn, they will only wear out that dog bone fast because it's doing most of the work.  I got to see my new stock dog bone that I polished up look old quick because I made that mistake.  They're all new now. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||d825077||10-07-2000||10:35 PM||||I got my ulitimate dogbone today and put it in my 1984 auto.  Now my whole car ratles and sounds like the engine is noiser at idle.  I hope I did not break something when I pulled the engine back to install the dog bone.||||reg||13
Z||000007||mbramble||10-08-2000||12:00 AM||||As Bob said in an above post, the Ultimate Dogbone transmits a lot more vibration to 4 cyl cars than the stock one with rubber bushings.  After hearing several others with results like yours, I replaced my worn out dogbone with a stock one last week ('87 2.5L Coupe).  And the tranny and engine mounts I ordered from the Fiero Store came in yesterday and I replaced them today.  Even after a new dogbone, I still had lots of movement with the engine.  Well, after seeing my motor mount today, I know why!!  There was hardly anything left of it -- looked like someone went after it with a hammer and chisel!!  The tranny mount looked good but went ahead and replaced it too.  Now there is very little engine movement.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 10-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||GT-maniac||10-08-2000||02:32 AM||||I'd have to disagree with bob here. I replaced the bushings in my V6's stock dog bone with poly ones and there is definately a lot more vibration in my car. It is a dead easy job though took me all of ten minutes tops.||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||10-08-2000||07:17 PM||||Vibration while moving is a non issue for this item. Even in the 4 cyl you won't notice the extra while moving.<P>At Idle tho it can make a big differance and, as Bob noted above, it matters what tranny you have. The automatic loads on the strut at idle in drive. Where a stick shift at idle has to be out of gear or the clutch pushed. Stick won't load the strut hardly at all when idling. My solution for automatics is put the car out of gear at long traffic lights.<P>If it's an automatic then it also matter what year it is. If you have an 86.5 or newer 4 cylinder with automatic, read the strut article in my cave.<P>I have an 87 4 cyl auto. I can tell you it does definatly transmit more vibration at idle in drive, allot more. However I don't have the air cleaner hitting the trunk wall anymore. The New OE rubber one I put in right after buying the car only lasted 6 months. The OE ones aren't ment for the loads this setup puts on them. (The best fix would be to retrofit the old dual trany mounts to the tranny instead of the single.)<P>The older automatics and all stick shifts have 2 tranny mounts. Those are OK with the OE strut if all the motor/tranny mounts are good. OE rubber ones will last a long time. Polly will transmit more vibration but shouldn't be as bad as the single mount automatics.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-08-2000).]||||reg||1
